      en: "The Yangtze River, with a total length of approximately 6,300 kilometers, is the longest river in Asia and the third longest in the world. Originating on the Plateau of Tibet in the Tanggula Mountains, it flows through eleven Chinese provinces and regions before emptying into the East China Sea near Shanghai. The river is a vital waterway and serves as the economic backbone of the nation, supporting roughly one-third of China's population. A major landmark along its course is the Three Gorges Dam, the world's largest power station, which is crucial for both electricity generation and flood control. Geographically, the river acts as a dividing line between northern and southern China and is home to unique biodiversity, including the endangered Yangtze finless porpoise. Historically, the river valley has been the cradle of numerous civilizations and a central trade route since ancient times. Despite intensive industrial development along its banks, there are increasing efforts directed toward environmental protection and restoring ecological balance.",
